-- title:
--   Proposition 3.4 — F = ⟨x, x₁⟩, and x_s g = g x_{s.g}, derivably from the relations (1) and (2)
-- statement:
--   $F$ is generated by $x = x_{\langle\rangle}$ and $x_1$, so $g$, $x_s$ and $x_{s.g}$ can always be written as words in them. If $g \in F$ and $s.g = t$ (`FinAct`: $g$ maps $s^\frown\xi$ to $t^\frown\xi$ for every infinite $\xi$), then $x_sg = gx_t$. Moreover, if $w_g$, $w_s$, $w_t$ are words in $x$ and $x_1$ representing $g$, $x_s$ and $x_t$, then the relator $w_sw_g(w_gw_t)^{-1}$, read with $x = x_{\langle\rangle}$ and $x_1 = x_1$, lies in the normal closure of the relations (1) and (2).
-- source:
--   Lodha, Y. and Moore, J. T., A nonamenable finitely presented group of piecewise projective homeomorphisms, Groups Geom. Dyn. 10 (2016) 177–200, https://doi.org/10.4171/GGD/347 (arXiv:1308.4250v3, whose page numbers are used), p. 6, Proposition 3.4

